[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s not often I get to show you something that genuinely feels different. This is the brand new Outlander and today you\'re probably the first people to see it. I\'ve been exploring Oregon in the Classic A camper waking up somewhere new each morning and today that journey has brought me here.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'d like to thank eTrailer for supporting this Oregon series. Their support makes trips like this possible and allows me to take you along as we explore some of the most unique trailers from all over the country. Come into this side of the Outlander.'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is where all the prep is going to take place. This table is huge. This drops down. It can stay in this configuration, but originally when they made this, they put'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in these braces that can hold this and give it more support. But on the current version, this is just a steel cable so you don\'t have to attach anything. On this table, you have two options. This is aluminum.'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They chose aluminum over stainless just because it\'s keeping the weight down. And then I think probably a better choice for most of us, you can match the HDPE that you have in the cabinets and'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the surfaces here and have that here as well. Just a lot easier to clean up and kind of like Escapod, very similar to the HDPE they use in their galley kitchens. You\'re now going to want some coverage from the elements. This is the'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Kammok awning. You\'ve seen it a lot. The beauty of having a rack on a trailer like this is that you can mount items like this. And this can come up in 3 seconds.'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I just tighten it here. I have all this area to prep. If I need a blender or a coffee machine, we have 12-V power here.'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'re going to see a fridge back here, but if that\'s not enough, there\'s room to put a fridge on the tongue. And you can also add a fridge that is built in in this section as well. That\'s an additional accessory.'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh if the wind comes up, like you guys know I love, you\'re just going to knock this down. I don\'t do it the way most people do. I push these two buttons, and I put it in like that. Again, this'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'table\'s going to come up really quickly, and I\'m ready on the road, and we don\'t need these because now they have that steel strap. Coming around to my favorite part on every trailer.'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First off, we have some really good seals here for dust intrusion. Those of you who own a camper know why that\'s important. You\'re just going to turn this, and it\'s on gas struts.'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They originally had a bit stronger struts on this, and they derated it so you can have some play with the sun and the height. So, I can give it a little extra push'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for my height, and also to ensure the sun gets cut off at certain angles. For those of you who are vertically challenged, they have attached this handle here. As you know for May, this'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will be perfect. So, you\'re going to see this is a really satisfying close. You can do it strong. Has that nice automotive feel to it. But, you also can just push this down, and just it\'s a click.'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Obviously, we want this back open so we can talk about my favorite part, the galley. The first thing we\'ll do is just turn on this light right here, and then there\'s a light up top as well. Now, the entire cooking area is lit.'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to take you to this top cabinet. This top cabinet on the base model would be empty. Tons of space to store things, but you can see it\'s a bit limited here, and that\'s because there'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is a 13-gallon water tank. And you can even upgrade to a 30-gallon tank as well. I\'ll show you where that fills outside here in a bit. You\'ll also see there\'s a little bit'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'taken up here as well. If you come to this side, this is another storage area and a tiny bit of it is taken up by an AC. Again, you do not need that AC. With the AC and'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water tanks removed, that\'s opening up all this space. You know, there\'s always a trade-off in these trailers. From here, if you did have a water tank, which I think a lot of people probably'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will go with because it\'s really smart, this is not plumbed in the way most of us are used to in North America. There isn\'t a water pump. This is a gravity-fed tank.'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, it\'s all running from gravity. The beauty of this is you have less failure points in the field. In the winter, there\'s no winterizing. You just drain this tank. Under here,'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you have a 7-gallon, what I call a gray tank. I like theirs better. They\'re calling it a holding tank. This will hold your gray water until you can dump it properly. For the gray water and the fresh water, we have level indicators.'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, it\'s all electronic. This is fresh. This one\'s your holding amount. Over here, we have two 120 inputs. We have two USB-C inputs. And then next, we probably need to talk'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about this surface. This is that HDPE surface we talked about, same that you\'ll see in trailers like Escapod\'s galley. You have a Dometic two-burner stove. This one doesn\'t have the glass top. I'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would add that if I was to purchase this. That way it gives you all this for prep area if you\'re not using the stove. And then this sink, I\'ll give you a different angle here. It is really deep.'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This isn\'t an RV sink. This is a home-style sink. Some of you more observant viewers are going to ask what this is. This is a port that allows the AC to drain into'

[05:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sink. And you\'re going to see this AC it functions more like a built-in home AC, but he\'s actually using a portable unit. It\'s a pretty neat idea. I\'m going to quickly highlight this'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bottom row. This is that 7-gallon gray tank. This is the built-in fridge, and over here is more storage plus access to your propane lines. Besides the tank, it\'s kind of hard to see down here. This is your drain valve.'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then down here, we have the small cap that allows the gray water out into gray water catches. And then you have this large cap. I\'m just going to call it the let\'s get out of here cap, and'

[06:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just let\'s everything dump through. As you can see, this isn\'t the largest fridge, but that\'s why they have an additional spot to put a fridge built in over here. And they\'ve tied in'

[06:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electrical to the front tongue box, so you can use a weather sealed fridge like my Iceco APL 55 or an Engel. Um but for many people who carry an ice chest, this is all you\'re going to need. This is'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just a little extra refrigeration that I think is definitely going to help. While this is primarily for a little extra storage, you also have an access panel here for your propane line, one to the Propex heater that is in here, and'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the other to your stove. One of the things you\'ll notice with the Outfitter, when it came to designing this, they wanted it to look and function a bit more like an automobile. So, even like the trim you\'re seeing'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, how the running lights are built in, the rain gutters, very reminiscent of things coming out of the auto industry. And just like your automobile, this has a 2-in receiver. This rear receiver is'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to allow you to carry bikes back here. But the issue is that hatch will get in the way of the bikes. So, you\'re going to need to add a swing-out bike rack. They\'re much more common now. You will find ways to work around galley'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kitchens like this and still get to bring the bikes. On this side, we have a bit of a blank canvas. So, this is just an equipment rack. These holes here are M8 threads. And so, you can put M8 bolts in here to'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'put MOLLE panels, put up your shovels, your traction mats. You name it, you can bring it here. This, even though it is looking like something I cannot stand'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on, it is a standing fender. And this fender will get you up to that roof, again with that roof rack that\'s built in. And then this little guy that you\'re'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seeing here, this is the water fill for that 13 or 30-gallon tank, whichever one you go with. Coming down here, we have the suspension. So, these 30-in tires are sitting on 15-in rims attached to'

[08:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Timbren 2000 HD suspension. So, this is independent suspension. Most of us are used to this in North America. It just means that each wheel is riding and moving independently of the other'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because they\'re not sharing that through axle. &gt;&gt; One of the features you\'ll see next is the front rack. I couldn\'t help but think how well my Dometic GO water jug would fit there. Years ago, I picked one up and I wasn\'t that impressed, but'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'after wrestling with 6-gallon water jugs over the years, I\'ve really come to appreciate that 2.5-gallon size. It\'s easier to carry, easier to pour, built like a tank, and has a handy little'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flow-reducing insert. If you do want running water, it still pairs well with the GO faucet. I\'m also seeing more and more campers replace one large jerry can with two of these. As usual, you can find it at etrailer.com linked in the'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'description below. &gt;&gt; Up on the roof, we have a large solar panel in the center here. I\'m going to say like 200 W. You have your fan in the front. In the back, we have the air intake for the AC.'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then this rack goes all the way around so you can add your own rooftop tents, other gear, other attachments, your Kamik awnings, your Kamik outposts, any of that stuff.'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'On the tongue platform, we have a number of things. When this trailer first came out, they had all the electrical in here. It\'s actually in this version, but the current model, all the electrical is housed in the cabin.'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'What you\'re going to get if you get this platform, uh on the base model, it\'s just the tongue. This is an optional upgrade, so is the spare tire. What this is is a pass-through storage. So, there is a'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'door just like this on the other side so you can get items out from either side, whichever they\'re closest to. And then up here, you have a carrying area for wood. Um this is a great place to strap down your additional fridge. This has'

[10:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electrical running from the cabin into here to power that fridge. Back here, we have a spare tire mount. It is beefy. It\'s also an add-on. And then up front, we have the propane, which is going to'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'run the Propex heater and the stove in the back. When I first tried to open this, I couldn\'t get it open. I thought the door was locked, but it actually has an automotive seal. So, you push the door in slightly, and it\'s going to open this up, again just giving'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a better weather seal. When I come in here, I flip this once and I get a daylight bulb. Flip it again, and it turns orange on the same rocker switch.'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then in here, I have another light to light up the cabin. You might not be able to see, maybe in my B-roll, this has more of that automotive, almost airline style lines, um the trim. It just has a different feel in here. I\'m'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to go through everything it has to offer, but just know both sides are pretty mirrored, his and her, but they do have different accessories, and I\'ll explain that. So, both have coat hooks, hat hooks. Both have these bungee'

[11:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nettings to hold extra gear. These have recessed space here. So, you can keep keys and bedtime items. They have cup holders. What I really like is'

[11:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on that side you have 120 AC ports. So, you can put your CPAP there and get it in that recessed area, make a support to hold it up. On this side you have USB ports. We have our Propex heater'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controls right here. Speaking of Propex, this is your air intake. Down at your feet is the air output. On the other side here you have a couple of rocker lights just like you do here.'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Up here, this is all storage and it\'s deep quite a bit back. Same thing on this side, but it\'s a little bit shallower because of your'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water tank. Here\'s that AC I was mentioning. So, this is a Zero Breeze. You could probably fit EcoFlow up there, too. And the beauty of this, it is ported so that it\'s pulling air in off the roof. You\'re'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to see that. And then air is obviously coming up and filling the cabin. And then any of that moisture it\'s pulling out, there is a hose that goes down into that sink that we talked'

[12:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about. As for the bed, it is a queen on this spot. So, your upper body is on a queen. Here it\'s a bit narrower where this plastic is. So, this is kind of designed more like a bed for a van. I'

[13:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think it\'s 49 in here wheel to wheel down below. If you do want a true queen or you want to raise this mattress up 4 in, they can remove this plastic and that\'s an option as well. I probably should'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'point out this window back here, a big beautiful window. You have the windows on the doors as well. We probably need to talk about construction. The construction of this is fiberglass.'

[13:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can You want to look at it? Come look. Come look. Let\'s talk construction. This, although it\'s fiberglass, it\'s a little different than many of us know. It\'s not a one-piece'

[13:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shell. It\'s not fiberglass panels. Underneath this is an aluminum frame. It\'s six sections of fiberglass that are joined together with M6 bolts. And then where it is joined,'

[13:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re using polyurethane to seal that, um, from elements, from the water and things like that. So, they\'re not, at the end, taking those seams and bonding them. They\'re bonded with hardware and then protected'

[14:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with polyurethane. Uh, what you\'re seeing here, they\'re making stamps in this, probably mainly for design, for beauty, but the stamps also probably give it a bit of strength.'

[14:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh, you\'re going to also see here, we got a coating like polyurethane, Raptor Liner, same thing that we\'re finding on this truck bed. They made this to keep the cost down, and it just gave them a'

[14:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trailer that they can sell at an affordable price by doing the construction this way. And just because it\'s different doesn\'t mean it\'s less strong. &gt;&gt; Cons: bolt-on chassis. In North America,'

[14:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people tend to expect a welded chassis. But in Europe, where this design philosophy comes from, bolted construction is often preferred. If a section of the trailer frame is damaged,'

[14:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can simply replace that component instead of repairing the entire frame. Next, interior feel. One of my biggest issues with many composite fiberglass trailers is that they can feel a little sterile inside. I\'d love to see an'

[15:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'optional warmer interior trim and lighting package. Fridge compatibility. If you already own a favorite 12-V fridge, there\'s a good chance it won\'t fit because of the shallow galley and storage compartments.'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Yes, you could probably make it work in the tongue box, but then you\'d be giving up a lot of valuable storage. Speaking of storage, it\'s on the modest side. I\'ve definitely seen less, but this'

[15:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trailer is aimed more at the campers who pack light or split their gear between their trailer and the tow vehicle like I do. Pros. Excellent prep space. This is probably my favorite feature. My Bean has a large side table and I use it constantly. On'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this trailer, it flips down instantly, doesn\'t need support legs, and gives you a ton of usable work space. It\'s hard to overstate how valuable extra counter space is on a small camper. Gravity-fed water. I love the simplicity'

[16:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of a gravity-fed system. The only trade-off is you\'ll need to climb onto the trailer or use a small step stool to refill it. Composite construction. &gt;&gt; Fiberglass, aluminum, advanced polymers, stainless steel, galvanized steel, no'

[16:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wooden products. &gt;&gt; No worries about wood, rot, mold, or mildew. That\'s one of the biggest long-term advantages of this style of trailer. Then you have price. &gt;&gt; The base model starts at $26,000.'

[16:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this version does not include propane stove and water system. And it\'s for people who normally use portable equipment such as portable stove and sink. Um the base model that includes'

[16:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'both propane system and water system starts at $28,000. &gt;&gt; At this price you see on the screen, I wouldn\'t call it a budget trailer, but'

[16:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you compare it to other off-road fiberglass trailers with a rear galley, I don\'t know of anything else offering this combination of features for less. You could compare it to a fiberglass square drop, but I really don\'t think'

[17:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s a fair comparison. This trailer has much more intentional styling and a lot more personality. It doesn\'t feel like just a fiberglass box. It feels like it has its own identity. One trailer never tells the whole story.'

[17:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Once you start comparing them side by side, the strengths and compromises of each become a bit more obvious. I\'ll link my fiberglass trailer playlist here, along with a few off-road fiberglass trailer reviews to help with'

[17:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that comparison. Thanks for watching and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
